<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="L-alanine biosynthesis II" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="ALANINE-SYN2-PWY" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; |FRAME: L-ALPHA-ALANINE| is an essential component of protein. In bacteria |FRAME: L-ALPHA-ALANINE| is also an important component of peptidoglycan (although the latter contains roughly 3 times more D-alanine than |FRAME: L-ALPHA-ALANINE|). Only about a tenth of total alanine synthesized is incorporated into peptidoglycan. At least three pathways (|FRAME: ALANINE-VALINESYN-PWY|, |FRAME: ALANINE-SYN2-PWY|, and |FRAME: PWY0-1021|) are known for production of |FRAME: L-ALPHA-ALANINE|. Production of |FRAME: L-ALPHA-ALANINE| from pyruvate via an aminotransferase is the main route for |FRAME: L-ALPHA-ALANINE| production is humans, and one of several routes operating in bacteria |CITS: [2890623]|.</Description>
